            description: 'Two poems, not one poem in two languages, and the difference matters. The Sumerian runs to something over four hundred lines: Inanna abandons her temples, dresses in the seven divine offices, and goes down; at each of seven gates a garment or ornament is taken from her until she stands naked before her sister Ereškigal, who fixes her with the eye of death and hangs her corpse on a hook. Enki makes two sexless creatures from the dirt under his fingernail, who win her body back by pitying Ereškigal\'s labour pains; but no one leaves the netherworld without a substitute, and the demons take Dumuzi, whose sister Geštinanna at last agrees to spend half of each year in his place. The Akkadian is about a hundred and forty lines, colder and faster: Ištar threatens to break the door and let the dead out among the living; while she is below, no bull mounts a cow and no man a woman; Ea makes a creature to trick Ereškigal, and the water of life is sprinkled. Its ending, on the washing of Tammuz, is disputed. Both titles above are given in transliteration.',
